 C/TR:,NAME,
C===========================================================
C C/TR - convert a real to an integer, truncating toward zero
C
C   Calling convention (single arg, in ACC):
C     ACC = real value    ->    ACC = integer value
C
C Subtracting the integer zero aligns the mantissa to the
C integer scale, so the fraction is shifted out; AMX does it on
C the absolute value and AVX puts the sign back, which is what
C makes the truncation go toward zero.  This is the Pascal
C runtime's P/TR (paslib/p_tr.dtran) plus the closing AEX: an
C integer word here carries no exponent, so the one left behind
C by the alignment has to be stripped.  That AEX also leaves the
C mode register at 7 - normalization and rounding suppressed,
C omega logical - which is what compiled code expects of an
C integer result on return.
C===========================================================
                ,NTR ,3      . suppress normalization and rounding
            15  ,ATX ,       . save the value for its sign
            1   ,AMX ,11B    . =I0  ACC = |value|, fraction gone
            15  ,AVX ,       . negate if the value was negative
            1   ,AEX ,11B    . =I0  strip the integer exponent
            13  ,UJ  ,
                ,END ,
